                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#texts"} Texts
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#formalization_libraries"} Formalization libraries
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relation_between_higher_topos_theory_and_univalent_type_theory"} Relation between higher topos theory and univalent type theory
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em univalent foundations for mathematics
            refers to any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundational system
            in which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universes"} universes
            are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifiers"} object classifiers
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
          %p
            Specifically, this applies to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+foundations"} mathematical foundations
            in (a)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universes"} universes
            – correspond to each other under the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax-semantics+duality"} syntax/semantics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+type+theory+and+category+theory"} relation between type theory and category theory
          %p
            Here,
            %em univalent foundations
            is in contrast to other (earlier) approaches of laying foundations in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theories"} type theories
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+of+propositions"} universe of propositions
            or (only) a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            is reflected in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe"} universe
          %p
            The basic idea of univalent foundations is, simply, to allow for universes that reflect
            %em all
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inconsistencies"} inconsistencies
            of the form of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russell%27s+paradox"} Russell's paradox
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Girard%27s+paradox"} Girard's paradox
            – universes that reflect all those
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            that are “small” compared to the universe. It is this technical subtlety – due to which a
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            may not be reflected in a given universe (namely if it is too large) but if it is, then it is so essentially
            = succeed "quely" do
              %em uni
            = succeed "valent\u201D." do
              %em uni
          %p
            The term “univalent foundations” as such was coined by
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Voevodsky11"} Voevodsky 11
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#UFIAS12"} UFIAS 12
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+foundations"} mathematical foundations
            based on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+dependent+type+theory"} Martin-Löf dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
            imposed on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} type universe
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of) such “univalent type universes” in higher analogues of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"}> ∞-toposes
            ”) was observed (and published) earlier in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Lurie09"} Lurie 09, Sec. 6.1.6
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifiers"} object classifiers
            and attributed to yet earlier private conversation with
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Rezk"} Charles Rezk
          %p That both concepts,
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalent
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universes"} universes
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+dependent+type+theory"} type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifiers"} object classifiers
                in (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"}> elementary
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} ∞-toposes
          %p
            do indeed correspond to each other was understood from the beginning (see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Shulman12a"} Shulman 12a
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://home.sandiego.edu/~shulman/hottminicourse2012/03models.pdf#page=3"} lecture 3, slide 3
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Shulman12b"} Shulman 12b
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Shulman19"} Shulman 19
          %p Concretely, what makes univalent foundations univalent is the condition, respectively,
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                that there exists a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} type universe
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Type
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Type
                (also often denoted
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒰
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
                = succeed "," do
                  %em reflects types and their equivalences
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
                %em in
                that universe, them being
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+in+homotopy+type+theory"} equivalent as types
                is equivalent to them being equivalent (“
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"}> equal
                ”) as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
                of type
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Type
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Type
              %p
                the usual formal
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
                for this statement of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
                is the famous expression
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi A
                          %mo ≃
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mstyle{:mathcolor => "blue"}
                          %mtext equivalence as types
                      %munder
                        %mo ≃
                        %mstyle{:mathcolor => "blue"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mtext univalence
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %munder
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi A
                          %mo =
                          %mi B
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                          %mrow
                            %mstyle{:mathcolor => "blue"}
                              %mtext equivalence as terms
                          %mrow
                            %mstyle{:mathcolor => "blue"}
                              %mtext in the type universe
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\underset{
                      \\color{blue}
                      \\text{equivalence as types}
                      }{
                      (A \simeq B)
                      }
                      \\underset{
                      \\color{blue}
                      \\;\;\text{univalence}\;\;
                      }{
                      \\simeq
                      }
                      \\underset{
                      \\color{blue}
                      \\text{equivalence as terms}
                      \\atop \color{blue}
                      \\text{in the type universe}
                      }{
                      (A = B)
                      }
            %li
              %p
                that there exists an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifier"} object classifier
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Object
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Object
                which
                = succeed "," do
                  %em reflects objects and their equivalences
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
                them being
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} equivalent as objects
                is equivalent to their
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+maps"} classifying maps
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Object
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Object
                being equivalent (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"}> homotopic
                ).
          %h3#in_higher_topos_theory In higher topos theory
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object+classifier"} object classifier
            (in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
          %h3#in_homotopy_type_theory In homotopy type theory
          %p
            Voevodsky’s univalent foundations (
            %a{:href => "#UFIAS12"}> UFIAS 12
            ) are an extension of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f%27s+dependent+type+theory"} Martin-Löf's dependent type theory
            obtained by adding:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+truncations"}> propositional truncations
                \.
          %p Notice that:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+extensionality"} function extensionality
                is implied by the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+resizing"} propositional resizing
                is sometimes also included as an axiom;
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UniMath"} UniMath
                library also uses
                = succeed "," do
                  %em type in type
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiom
                that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} type universe
                contains itself. Strictly speaking, this makes the system
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inconsistency"} inconsistent
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Girard%27s+paradox"} Girard's paradox
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
                is consistent with the univalent foundations of mathematics and may be assumed separately.
          %h2#OverviewOfTheBasicConcepts Overview of the basic concepts
          %p
            A map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to B
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            if it has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            and a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retraction"} retraction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
            asserts that for any two types
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo :
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B:\mathcal{U}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A=B)\to (A\simeq B)
            obtained via the induction principle of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"} identity type
          %p
            In the univalent foundations, one can distinguish between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property"} property
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            (in general,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+structure"} higher structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+level"} homotopy levels
            that starts at the level of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible+type"} contractible types
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "sans-serif"}
                    %mrow
                      %mi is
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi equiv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathsf{is{-}equiv}(f)
            is a proposition in the above sense.
          %p
            Types of which the identity types are propositions are called sets. Many familiar types, such as the types of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            are sets in this sense, and in many subjects of algebra, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            is also restricted to sets, because there are surjective maps between other types that provably do not split. For instance, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+cover"} double cover
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            has no section, while each of its fibers is inhabited by two points. We say that sets are of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+level"} homotopy level
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
            if its identity types are of homotopy level
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            By the univalence axiom, we often have nice characterizations of “the type of a certain kind of thing”. For instance, the type of
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            (over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topology"} discrete
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+linear+group"} general linear group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi GL
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} GL(n)
            = succeed "-element" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+group"} symmetric group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+order"} cyclically ordered
            = succeed "-element" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+group"} cyclic group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} C
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{C}_n
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence+axiom"} univalence axiom
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UniMath+project"} UniMath project
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+homotopy+type+theory"} modal homotopy type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+structures"} higher structures
          %h2#references References
          %h3#in_higher_topos_theory_2 In higher topos theory
          %ul
            %li#Lurie09
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              , Section 6.1.6 of:
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
              (attributed there to
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Rezk"} Charles Rezk
          %h3#in_type_theory In type theory
          %h4#texts Texts
          %p
            Comprehensive introduction motivated form the concept of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry"} symmetry
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Bezem"}> Marc Bezem
